In article <7slngc$toq$1@Mercury.mcs.net>, les@Mercury.mcs.net says...
> In article <MPG.1252a1b6cab2848e9896d9@news.bctel.ca>,
> K. Bjarnason  <kelseyb@bc.sympatico.no.spam.ca> wrote:
> 
> >All of which is to say, an application _does_ pick "the version it 
> >wants" _where such versioning is relevant_ and otherwise simply gets the 
> >latest version.  Short of an actual DLL bug, though, the application 
> >should keep right on working unless its (the app's) author did something 
> >wrong.
> >
> >So what's the problem?
> 
> What!!?? A DLL bug?  No... That can't happen. No one would ever
> release a DLL with bugs and then update it with another of
> the same name with different bugs.  For the reality-impaired,
> why do you think the DLL is updated in the first place if
> some operation is not changed in a way that some application
> requires?  That means that either the app that needs the change
> is using undocumented features or the DLL was not working
> as documented.

Or the API provided by the DLL has been _extended_.  Or the operations 
have been _enhanced_ (eg faster copies and searches).  Or...

There are any number of reasons why such a DLL would be updated 
_without_ being either a bugfix or a change in the manner existing 
applications use the DLL.

Let's use a hypothetical DLL I hypothetically wrote as an example.  It 
has one function, as follows:

Function: SortArrayOfIntegers

Prototype:
int	SortArrayOfIntegers( int array[], const size_t elements );

Required Header:
"SortIntAr.h"

Purpose:
The SortArrayOfIntegers function takes an array of integers and sorts 
the contents of the array such that the contents of each element compare 
greater than or equal to the contents of the preceeding element.

Inputs:
The first parameter is the array to be sorted.  The second parameter is 
the number of elements to sort.

Outputs:
The first parameter is modified in-place.  Because of this, passing 
const arrays, or pointers to const arrays, will cause a fault.  Upon 
successful completion, the array passed as the first parameter will be 
in sorted order.

Resources:
Given an array of N elements, the function may require allocation of 
temporary storage up to sqrt(N) elements, and stack space up to log(N) 
elements.  No other storage is required.

Return values:
Upon completion, SortArrayOfIntegers returns zero for success or less 
than zero for error.  The possible errors are represented by symbolic 
constant in the supplied header file, and they are:

ERR_INTSORTARR_TOOFEW
This indicates there were too few elements to sort; that is, zero or one 
elements.

ERR_INTSORTARR_TOOMANY
This indicates there were too many elements to sort.  If the element is 
very large (>= 25% of available memory) then temporary storage overhead 
may become too high to sort the array.  Not available on all platforms.

ERR_INTSORTARR_ILLEGALSIZE
This indicates that the value of the second parameter exceeded the 
actual number of elements in the array.  Not available on all platforms.

ERR_INTSORTARR_ILLEGALCONST
This indicates that the array passed is a constant array and cannot be 
modified.  Not available on all platforms.

Notes:

On some platforms, not all error conditions can be trapped; on such 
platforms, the conditions noted as "Not available on all platforms" will 
generally cause an application fault.



So far so good.  You get the header and the DLL "intsort.dll" and you 
use them in your application.  You're currently using version 1.0, and 
your application works.

Now I release version 2.0.  Guess what?  There's still only one 
function, it still has the same interface, it still reports the same 
errors under the same conditions; in short, the documentation need not 
change *one iota*.

Hmm... so why did I release 2.0?  Simple; the first version used a 
bubble sort.  Sure, it worked, but for anything more than a handful of 
elements, it was *slow*.  Version 2.0 uses a mergesort.

Now, your application, which used the function to sort a largish array 
in one thread while doing initialization in another thread, stops 
working properly, because you were relying on the observed - but not 
documented - O(N^2) sort times.  You knew you had plenty of time to do 
your other functions, because the sort took such a long time.

With version 2.0, your application breaks.  Why?  Simple; you relied on 
undocumented behaviour.  Since that behaviour was not documented, and 
therefore you cannot rely upon it to remain consistent, your design is 
broken - not my DLL.

Voila.  An updated DLL, with no bugfix, with no change in documented 
behavior, which any app developer using the DLL will *happily* accept as 
it is, without requireing them to change a single line of code to get a 
considerable increase in efficiency... except you, who relied upon 
features not documented.

Now, I should cry for you why, exactly?




