> replaces the system DLL's with ones that optimize their own
> access and degrade or break the competition.  

Funny; that DLL I proposed would not break *any* application, regardless 
of who wrote it - as long as the application actually used the 
_published_ effects.  Therefore, upgrading that DLL will optimize _any_ 
application using it - as long as the developer of said app has the 
merest shred of a clue.

> However, games
> are only fun when playing field is level.  Can any application
> vendor get away with this or just Microsoft?

Anyone at all, of course.  There's absolutely nothing stopping you 
writing a DLL.  The only thing stopping you releasing a new version, 
with the same name, but a changed (not extended) API, or significantly 
different effects, things which would break applications wihch used your 
published API and effects, is that doing so would get you laughed at.

Yes, bugs happen, but intentional breakage will simply cost you your 
user base.




