>
>What I've seen in older days was this approach: put the DLL in the
>same directory as the app. Say we have to incompatible DLL's, lets
>name them MFC30_a.DLL and MFC30_b.DLL - neither the _a nor
>the _b shows up in the filename, though.
>
>App A works best with MFC30_a.DLL, and this DLL is put beside the
>App A. App B works with MFC30_b.DLL, but it won't work with the
>(older) MFC30_a.DLL. Now load app B - it loads MFC30_b.DLL from
>the system directory, and everything works fine. Load app A, and it
>links to MFC30_b.DLL, and still it works (or app A shows a few minor
>glitches). Unload both apps, and now load app A.
>
>App A loads MFC30_a.DLL and works fine. Now start app B, and it is
>dynamically linked to MFC30_a.DLL - and this won't work. No chance
>for app B to load "its" correct DLL.
>

God, I am *SO* f*cking sick of hearing this bullsh*t! This is *NOT*
how DLLs work in 32-bit Windows! Each app would link with its own copy
of the DLL. I encourage anyone with a modicum of programming skill to
try it for themselves.

It amazes me that this lie is still propagated after so many years,
especially considering that it takes about three minutes to verify
that it's totally false.

If you're reading this and you have access to a Windows development
system and an interest in the truth, check it out for yourself:

-----------
JUNKLIB.CPP
-----------

#include <windows.h>
#include <stdio.h>
static HANDLE s_hDLL;
BOOL APIENTRY DllMain (HANDLE hDLL, DWORD, LPVOID) {
{
    s_hDLL = hDLL;
    return TRUE;
}
void __declspec (dllexport) PrintDLLPath (void) {
    CHAR fn [8192];
    GetModuleFileNameA ((HMODULE) s_hDLL, fn, 8192);
    printf ("junklib path: %s\n", fn);
}

-----------
JUNKEXE.CPP
-----------

#include <windows.h>
extern void __declspec (dllimport) PrintDLLPath (void);
void main (void) {
    PrintDLLPath ();
    Sleep (INFINITE);
}

------------
Instructions
------------

Build JUNKLIB.DLL and JUNKEXE.EXE. Copy both files into each of two
empty directories. Run JUNKEXE.EXE from both directories
simultaneously. Note that the printed paths are *DIFFERENT*!



