You are right on this. For now (but not in the future).

> [...]
> Build JUNKLIB.DLL and JUNKEXE.EXE. Copy both files into each of two
> empty directories. Run JUNKEXE.EXE from both directories
> simultaneously. Note that the printed paths are *DIFFERENT*!

You are right on this, too. Sorry, I tested this with my old 16-Bit-DLL's,
and it works like I told you. I tested it with 32-Bit-DLL's, and it works
as you told it. My apologies, but my time of programming for Windows
is over, and I didn't try it for a long time.

But still, I haven't seen this practice (putting the DLL in the apps dir) a
lot - it seems most apps won't do it, which leaves everyone without a
CS degree standing in the rain. This is the fault of M$ - to cite you:

"I don't think it's reasonable to expect people to have CS degrees in
order to use computers."

But it is the case, for Windows, in this scenario.

Seems this solution doesn't belong to common knowledge (others
have told me the same false claims that I made previously). Most
installers install their DLL in the system dir, anyway. But I agree
that this is the fault of the app vendor. And it is only partly the
fault of M$, because this knowledge is well hidden in the docs
(but after reading your solution, I found it).

I think the reason that M$ has kludges like the "smart repair" in
Windows 2000 is due to the fact that most vendors don't know or
don't care (Windows 2000 can restore the most up-to-date DLL in
case an installer overwrites it with an older version). Still, this
"feature" might break a newly installed app.

I'm sorry for posting wrong informations to this newsgroup.

Ciao,
Volker





