As you know, Linux doesn't have kernel debugger.
So most powerful debugging tool is 'printk' for now.
It's not productive and a kind of drudgery to play with it.
...
Briefly.
Linux kernel debugger for alpha has been finished.
It's a mixture of SGI's Linux kernel debugger for Intel and
David mosberger's Alpha Linux kernel debugger and some sugar on top.

SGI version strongly depends on Intel CPU's features that doesn't
exist in alpha.
Alpha's feature for debugging is BPT instruction only.
And SGI doesn't use 'int $3'(Intel's BPT) 'cause there're a lot
more powerful and useful CPU features.

So, SGI's interface, keyboard driver and kernel symbol manipulation
functions were useful for alpha.
David mosberger's version was the first kernel debugger for alpha.
But It's not being used and is a remote debugger that needs another
system to debug it.
I excerpt a few functions from it, like next instruction prediction
for single step and debugger entry etc.

So now, New alpha linux kernel debugger has most the features of
SGI version and a few more features.
You can get into kernel debugger anytime by pressing 'Pause' key
and put breakpoints, do single step, single step over,
process stack trace, ps, memory dump and modify,  disassemble ,
register dump and modify etc.

MP is not supported, My main platform that I'm working on is not MP.
You can find the codename of the platform somewhere in the capture.

It'll be on API(www.alpha-processor.com) home page,
I don't know when it would be there.
