Hello,

Here's the problem I'm having.  I am trying to install
Red Hat Linux 6.0 on an Alpha system, a 433a Digital Personal Workstation.
The system has 2 4Gb hard drives.  I have NT installed on the first drive,
and would like to keep it there.  I have wiped the second drive, and want
to install linux there.

I've followed the rather convoluted and disorganized directions for
installing on the Alpha, provided with the Red Hat Boxed set.

I've updated the AlphaBIOS firmware to rev 5.69.

I created a milo boot disk, using the miata image.

I added the milo disk to my os list, and I've been able to boot into
milo, off the floppy.

I then used milo to boot of the linux boot disk (made with generic.img).
It did take me quite a while to figure out how to do this, the manuals
are very vague at this critical point.  However, I finally figured out
to use the command:
        MILO> boot fd0:vmlinux.gz root=/dev/fd0 load_ramdisk=1

I went into Disk Druid to make partitions.

I created partitions for linux on my second drive, /dev/sdb.

I created a 2Mb dos partition, and called it /dosutils.

I created a 128Mb swap partition.

I created a single large ext2 partition with / as the mount partition, on
which to install linux.  This partition is therefore something like 3.87 Gb.

I then was asked to format the swap partition, which I said yes to, and also
said yes to check for bad blocks.

I then was asked to choose which packages to install.  I chose "everything".

It then asked to format the / partition, I said yes, and also asked it to
check for bad blocks.

At this point it seems to hang, and never finish.  I actually went to bed while
it appeared to still be working, and it still had the same screen up in
the morning:  "Running" and "Making ext2 filesystem on /dev/sdb6"

So, the formatting of my linux partition never finishes.

Any ideas?

Also, it never asked to format the little dos partition, which I created in order
to install milo, etc.  When do I format this guy?

Jason



