Starting at:
http://msdn.microsoft.com/winlogo/default.asp

Download "The Designed for Windows NT and Windows 98 Logo Handbook"


We see, among other things...

Core components are defined as DLLs that application developers have 
come to depend on for the proper functionality of their applications. 
Reverting to an older version or to an incorrectly implemented version 
may break many third party applications.  In general terms, the core 
components are USER, GDI, and KERNEL. Core components are files which 
are installed by the operating system during a full installation, or 
which are considered operating system upgrades (for example, DirectX�, 
Direct3D�). This includes such things as DLLs which are installed by 
applets. This list is dynamic. For the most up to date list of core 
components for Windows NT 4.0 and Windows 98, see 
http://msdn.microsoft.com/developer/winlogo/downloads.htm.

Also:

Any files which are installed by an application, but might be shared by 
multiple applications. Shared components are usually DLLs, but may be 
EXEs or any other file type. These must be "refcounted" unless they are 
"core components" or installed to the application's own directory.

Also:

Uninstall: Applications must completely uninstall from a system by means 
of an automated, registered uninstaller.  Applications must not remove 
core components or shared components necessary to other applications.

Also:

Core components must not be refcounted in the registry by application 
installers. Please see http://www.veritest.com/ftp/core.htm for the most 
up-to-date and complete list of Windows 98 and Windows NT 4.0 core 
components.

Also:

Required:
The vendor must run Pretest the application using VeriTest's Install 
Analyzer & include the report results when you submit your product to 
VeriTest.  Improper handling of core and shared components is the number 
one reason applications fail Logo testing on the first try and must 
retest.  The Install Analyzer provides automated testing of compliance 
in this area. The Install Analyzer can be found at 
http://www.veritest.com/mslogos/nt98/analyzer.htm 


And on and on.

All of this is both freely available and commonly known, and the install 
tools (InstallShield, etc) typically also document the core component 
handling requirements and shared component requirements, usually with a 
suggestion to see the MS site for more info.

What does all this mean?  Simple: Windows protects against system 
corruption about the same way your TCP/IP stack does - by established 
standard mechanisms for accomplishing things.  When somone - through 
malice or stupidity - violates those rules, bad things can happen.  
Install developers, and serious vendors in general, try not to get raps 
as "system manglers", and they do this by following the established 
guidelines.

Also take a look at:

http://support.microsoft.com/support/kb/articles/Q222/1/93.ASP

This documents some of the features of the new MSI installer with its 
Windows File Protection.





