OK. First of all, you have an working aboot, congratulation if you have
access to some other alpha running linux you made it (almost). If
possible put a working kernel (can be found at
ftp.lur.rwth-aachen.de/pub/incoming/2.2.12.gz this one supports romfs,
which is necessary for recent redhat ramdisks) on a bsd partition (best
made with minlabel from the aboot package) that resides under the 2gb
barrier. You can boot than into ramdisks by typing
"'number_of_kernel_containing_partiton'/'kernel_name' root=/dev/fd0
load_ramdisk=1" (this works surely with rh 5.0 and rh6.0 ramdisk).
Number_of_kernel_containing_partition means 1 for a:, 2 for b:, and so
on (I guess the limit on bsd partitions is eight). Kernel_name is the
name of the kernel e.g. 2.2.12.gz.  
> I have tried the bootdisk.1.3.72.jensen.srm and a custom kernel (which I
> got from somewhere) also, in which case I have been able to boot the
> kernel, but the kernel has not been able to find a valid ramdisk. (kernel
> fails with the message "unable to find valid ramdisk starting from sector
> 0")
What ramdisk did you use in combination with what kernel to get this
far? It might be that the kernel does not contain the used filesystem
type of the ramdisk. IIRC redhat 5 did not use romfs but 6.0 does, about
debian and suse I am not sure.
