>Yeah that DirectX reinstall is such a headache, all two and a half
>minutes of it.

Assuming it works; if it doesn't, well, you have to reinstall Windows in all
probability, because until you do, your video card will be the 4th Direct X
display driver, and the system will be unwilling to run in anything but
640x480 because the card you replaced isn't probing correctly (since it's
not in the system), even though the "device manager" says you're using the
new card.  Why?  Because some DLL, or some registry entry, is corrupt.  It's
faster to reinstall, on a games machine, than to figure out what's corrupt.

