> The problem is much more subtle than "supporting old interfaces".
> Whenever you update a library - even if it's just a harmless little
> bug fix adding no new interfaces - that library no longer supports the
> old interfaces exactly as it did before. Something about the library's
> behavior needed to change (otherwise why would there be an update?).
> So how do you know whether the change breaks some app? You don't,
> unless you actually test every app with your new library. Since that's
> practically impossible, the safety of your update can't be guaranteed.

what's your problem?  the point is that on unixy systems you can install 
a new version of a shared library without removing the old one.  the
implication is that apps that ran happily with the old version can
continue to do so, while new apps use te new version.  if the user so
disires, he probably can try his app with the new version, but he can
always fall back on using the old version.  all this without having to
go to special treatment of apps, like fooling around with the registry
or copying the dlls into the directory where the app resides (which will 
cause a maintenancenightmare when the dlll MUST BE REPLACED for whatever 
reason.

