> >In gnu.misc.discuss, les@Mercury.mcs.net (Leslie Mikesell) writes:
[snip]
> The point is, you *can't* resolve the conflict if another app needs
> a different DLL of the same name, whether they are different versions
> of the same thing or two different things entirely.  Once one of
> them is loaded, you can't load a different one from a different
> location without rebooting to get rid of the other one.

To quibble:

You actually *can* if you try hard enough, but nobody does. To do it,
you need to specify a full or partial path, not just a filename, when you
load the library. If you specify just the filename, the first thing Windows
does
is check for loaded libraries having same name.

The normal load-time linking procedure does it by filename (not path), so
90%
of the time you are going to get the already loaded library.

The trick to getting a different version is unloading the library, which is
most
cases not not require a reboot. (However, there are some libraries that the
system loads and never unloads. For those, you'll have to reboot.)






