> Daniel Johnson <daniel.n.johnson@worldnet.att.net> wrote:
> >I realise it isn't the nice clean naming convention you are used to, but
it
> >does the same thing- it indicates which API the thing supports.
>
> Except it obviously doesn't, because there are version changes that
produce
> incompatible libraries, which are not denoted in the name.

There are not *supposed* to be incompatible. I realise MS sometimes
screwes this up, but giving them a fancier, more systematic versioning
system wont help.

Surely they'll screw that up in the same way.

> >I have no idea whether GDI32.DLL from Win95 OSR1 would work if
> >inserted into Windows 98SE, but I rather suspect it would: As far as I've
> >seen, MS *has* been good about this particular .DLL, and they have not
> >changed the API- not even to extend it noticably.
>
> I didn't say OSR1 (that's 95a), I said *original* Win '95.

Oops. Do you know something about Win95's origional release
I don't?

> The point is, if *ANY* two versions of it are incompatible, then your
theory
> that this is somehow a meaningful "version number" breaks down.

In this case, as far as I know, there are no incomatibilities- all the GDI
revisions are compatible.

It's like minor revisions in Unix. You want to release enhancements and
especially bug fixes that will be inherited by existing programs. But you
need ot maintain a stable API while doing this, or those programs break.

The exact details of how the loader works aren't terribly important to that.

> >I could be wrong about that. They might be bug fixes in it that Win98SE
> >depends upon. Try it at your own risk. :D
>
> In other words, they *aren't* quite compatible, and they need to introduce
> version numbers, so the system can tell which version of GDI32 it needs.

They do have version numbers in the files, of course, but those function
as minor revision numbers do in Unix- every linking app gets the most
recent one available.

Microsoft has gone through the trouble to keep an older version of this
API around, in the form of GDI.DLL. This provides the 16 bit API, which
is different and incompatible. But they had to go through some trouble
to do this: you can't really have two GDI DLLs alive at once, so in actual
fact GDI32 just loads GDI and calls into it, so there's only one "real"
GDI.

This is exactly what you've been asking for: a new DLL with a new filename
for a new API- in spite of the difficulties this poses.







