The terminology is different, but the basic idea is very like Unix
major/minor
revisions.

I realise that the filename changes aren't called "version numbers", but
they *do* the same thing Unix .so major version number changes do- they
allow a program to request the API it wants to use when there
have been several, while it still gets the bug fixes and smaller upgrades
that can be fit in without breaking compatibility, In theory.

I see no reason to suppose that switching to a more systematic naming
scheme woudl make MS any better about compatibility.

It would be clearer if the things were labeled "GDI121.DLL" and
"GDI122.DLL", but it wouldn't make things any more stable. Everyone would
still bind to the most recent minor revision in their major revision, and if
they
will break because of that, they'll still break.






