Which is always a good point... see, M$ tries to dumb people down,
whereas Linux does not.

> >>Only if the authors realize that the library change breaks existing
> >>apps. Conflicts happen when the authors *DON'T* realize it, and
> >>neither Unix's nor Windows' scheme can protect against that.
> >
> >The difference is, if the author *does* know, Windows *still* doesn't
> >help.
> >
>
> No help is required. If the authors of a Windows shared library know
> that an update is not back-compatible, they can do exactly what they'd
> do under Unix - give the updated library a new name (e.g. MFC30 ->
> MFC40). That works just as well in Windows as it does in Unix.

I've never seen this in practice. And it wouldn't work.

What I've seen in older days was this approach: put the DLL in the
same directory as the app. Say we have to incompatible DLL's, lets
name them MFC30_a.DLL and MFC30_b.DLL - neither the _a nor
the _b shows up in the filename, though.

App A works best with MFC30_a.DLL, and this DLL is put beside the
App A. App B works with MFC30_b.DLL, but it won't work with the
(older) MFC30_a.DLL. Now load app B - it loads MFC30_b.DLL from
the system directory, and everything works fine. Load app A, and it
links to MFC30_b.DLL, and still it works (or app A shows a few minor
glitches). Unload both apps, and now load app A.

App A loads MFC30_a.DLL and works fine. Now start app B, and it is
dynamically linked to MFC30_a.DLL - and this won't work. No chance
for app B to load "its" correct DLL. Whom do you blame? Of course, the
vendor of app B, though its the fault of vendor A. You see, this way its
dependant from the order the DLL's are loaded.

Worse things will happen if Vendor A insists on installing "its" DLL in
the system directory - now app B stops working, because you can have
only ONE DLL of this kind. And I've seen quite a few DLL's with exactly
the same version number, but with different lengths.

In Linux (or UNIX), this is different. Because the shared libs are named
differently, app A can load "its" shared library, and app B can load "its"
shared library, too, at the same time (the name differs). Both app will
run fine. Of course, you will use more memory, because you have to
load both shared libs, but this will work.

And better: the comand ldd will show you which shared lib is used. You
can track down any problems real fast this way (this is a bit harder with
Windows, because M$ sometimes will  change DLL's, but won't change
the version number, so you won't get a clue). If you install app A and app
B stops working - how will you find out the reason for this?

BTW: the worst crap I've seen with DLL's concerns Windows 9x. There
is a 64Kb-Block in the registry devoted to storing registered DLL's complete
with path. If you install too much programs, or use too long path names,
this block will some day be filled up, and from then on, you can't install
new apps without deleting old ones (or re-installing old ones with shorter
path names...). Don't know if Windows NT has the same limits, though,
but it wouldn't surprise me...

One other thing: because you get the source code with Linux for most of
your apps, you can track this kind of problems (incompatibilities) much
faster. And if you can't, because you don't care for the source code, there
are enough people out there who can and will help you (and who will send
bug reports to the programmers, so this will be fixed fast). This is one of the

reason why you won't find these kind of problems in the real world...

Ciao,
Volker




