Chad Mulligan wrote in message ...

>You should download and read the getting results booklet.  User errors
>aren't broken software but broken wetware.


This is silly.  Most user errors--especially ones of the sort that you
described; manual recalculation of the table of contents was one--are quite
preventable by better program design.  Blaming the user is arrogant, makes
no sense, and will not advance the world of computer software.  Can you
think of any reason why the program should ever PRINT an inaccurate table of
contents?  Shouldn't the program update the TOC in background, and make sure
that it's done before ink hits the paper regardless?

I'm not saying that every tool should make you an expert in the field in
which you use it.  Lord knows there are lots of really ugly newsletters out
there thanks to home desktop publishing software.  But tools should not
require you to become an expert in the use of the tool, just to get around
the tool's deficiencies.  And no tool should provide you so easily with
results that are completely inaccurate or invalid.

---Michael B.






