hs@inferno.nirvananet (Hartmann Schaffer) wrote:

>> 
>> Come on now. The app author can simply install the required library
>> version in the directory holding the app's main executable.
>
>so if i have 5 apps that need a certain dll i have to install 5 copies
>of that dll in order to make sure that my apps don't break?
>

That's right. If you're an ISV and you wish to lock your app down to
one particular *MINOR* revision of a library, you *MUST* install a
private copy of that library on a Windows system. Unix definitely has
a potential sharing advantage here.

This is an extremely rare case, however. I believe that most ISVs
would want their products to benefit from field updates of the
libraries, so they'd do everything they could to get their products
working with the latest versions as of the product shipment date.

Finally, Unix's potential advantage in this scenario is clearly one of
storage consumption. Unix still doesn't have anything over Windows
when it comes to preventing shared library conflicts.



