In article <37e32c08.147791793@news.shore.net>,
Se�n � Donnchadha <sean@forget.about.it> wrote:

>>On Unix, an update will *not* "break" any installed application
>>because libraries are not overwritten.

>Wrong. On Unix, an update *CAN* break installed applications. That's
>because applications usually select DLLs through symbolic links which
>*ARE* overwritten during installation.

If my understanding is correct, the symbolic links only aide during the
compilation of a program.  During compilation, the real name of the
library is resolved.  Take a look at the following ldd output:

        libsocket.so.1 =>        /usr/lib/libsocket.so.1
        libnsl.so.1 =>   /usr/lib/libnsl.so.1
        libc.so.1 =>     /usr/lib/libc.so.1
        libdl.so.1 =>    /usr/lib/libdl.so.1
        libmp.so.2 =>    /usr/lib/libmp.so.2

If you were correct, shouldn't it have been?:

        libsocket.so =>        /usr/lib/libsocket.so
        libnsl.so =>   /usr/lib/libnsl.so
        libc.so =>     /usr/lib/libc.so
        libdl.so =>    /usr/lib/libdl.so
        libmp.so =>    /usr/lib/libmp.so

Note, the application was compiled/linked with -lnsl et al.  No
lib version specified.

Therefore, if libsocket is upgraded, the old version will still be
available for programs that were linked against it.  The libsocket.so
symlink is not even looked at.

Of course if someone replaced libsocket.so.1 with a different
libsocket.so.1, then there can be problems.  If I remember correctly
(please correct me if I am wrong), this type of problem did occur with
glibc2.0 -> glibc2.1.  The file libc.so.6 is used for both versions.
Shouldn't glibc2.1 used libc.so.6.1 instead so they can both
co-exist easily?

	--ewh
