>I think you are mistaken.  As I understand it, a company director is
>obliged by law to uphold the interests of that company's shareholders.
>But that does _not_ necessarily mean making money!
>
>I know that these days many people think that there is nothing else
>worth striving for, but believe me, they are mistaken.

More precisely, the director is obliged to uphold the interests of
shareholders (which *forcibly* mandates some very particular legal
requirements concerning tax/payroll filings and corporate reporting),
and to follow the direction of the things voted the body of
shareholders.

The sets of goals and politicking that can ferment beneath that set of
forcible requirements go against the notion of there being any single
clear mandate that could be named as *the* mandate.

Many constraints/objectives commonly get tied together such that the
naive could mistake "making money" to be the sole goal.

Even when that does seem to be the sole goal, considerable variations
in behaviour will fall out of the seemingly simple question:
   - Who is it that is supposed to be "making money?"
   - How is the notion of "making money" supposed to be evaluated?

Enter in confounding influences such as:
 - Executive option plans
 - Multiple classes of common stock, as well as preferred shares
 - What is the precise metric used to evaluate performance

And you can see both vast and sizable lawsuits as well as vast
variations in behaviour including behaviour that acts in the interests
of one group of shareholders and to the detriment of others.
