I just installed 6.0 on the same system.

I suggest you create the Generic Boot Disk and Ramdisk floppy
disks from the Images directory.  You can do this on an Intel
machine running DOS or Windows with rawrite in the DOSUTILS
directory if you don't already have a Linux system running
somewhere.

Switch the AS200 to the SRM console, selecting OSF as the
operating system.  You don't need ARC or MILO anymore for
this system (and I'm not sure it even works).  Even if
it does work the SRM console has the OSF PAL code and I
always find it easier to work with.

You should then be able to boot the generic boot disk
by just saying

boot fd0

or

boot dva0

from the SRM console prompt.  I didn't have to set any
flags or specify any files to load, the generic boot disk
has an aboot.conf file on it to automatically start things
up (You may have to edit this file later to kick start your
hard disk boot).

Don't use disk druid, use fdisk to partition the disk.  Start
your first partition (usually swap space) at cylinder 2 or 3
to leave empty space at the start of the boot disk for the
boot record (to be supplied later).

When you do update the boot record, some documentation tells
you to use vmlinux.gz or some other file.  I found that this
has all been replaced with one file called vmlinuz which is
a link to the actual controller.  In my case, the device I
installed to was SCSI unit 5, so my write boot command was

swriteboot /dev/sdd /boot/bootlx /boot/vmlinuz

Hope this helps some.

-- 
