>
>Pretty much _every_ modern Unix uses dynamically linked libraries.
>Where have you been?

Pedant point: that's "dynamically loaded". But you are correct;
apart from such archaic holdovers as SCO Xenix (and even they
might use DLLs), evern Unix has the equivalent of Microsoft's
.DLL -- in the case of Sun, they are '.so' files; in the case
of HP, they used to be '.sa' or '.sl' files (I think they're
switching over); in the case of Linux, they're '.so'.

As for selecting dynamic libraries -- the idea of a minor release
is to add functionality and/or correct bugs in a library (which
is why I'm somewhat less than thrilled with the current
glibc2.0/glibc2.1 effort; something's a bit strange in there), not
to completely break with tradition; major releases, of course,
have the option to break all existing code, should it be necessary.
(One would hope that the #include file for such a DLL has nice
big letters stating such, though! :-) )

Linux uses symbolic links, and is therefore susceptible to the
problem of a DLL being overwritten during install.  However,
it seems to me that this is less of a problem on Linux (and
Unix in general) than it might be on Windows; it may be in part
because of the fact that one can't open a text file [*] for write
if someone else has it open for execution; the system will return
ETXTBSY, "Text file busy"; this means that install programs
have to move it away instead of simply overwriting it.

Note also that, once a file is opened, the kernel refers to it
by inode rather than by name.

I would hope that Microsoft also has these safeguards.... :-)

>
>-- 
>FORTRAN, "the infantile disorder", by now nearly 20 years old, is hopelessly
>inadequate for whatever computer application you have in mind today: it is
>too clumsy, too risky, and too expensive to use.
>		-- Edsger W. Dijkstra, SIGPLAN Notices, Volume 17, Number 5

But it's still around. :-)

[*] Nomenclature confusion; in times of old, executables had three
    sections:  code, data, and block storage space (BSS).  Code was
    also referred to as text; both executables and DLLs are
    therefore text files, using this nomenclature.  Of course,
    reading these "text files" may be difficult in one's
    favorite text editor or word processor.  Sigh. :-)
    BSS is also interesting, in that it's supposed to be zero
    upon initial load; however, MS has the interesting habit
    (bug?  feature?) of not immediately unloading DLLs, which means
    its BSS might be crap in certain cases.  (MS is not alone;
    Apollo DOMAIN/Aegis had similar semantics in its uninitialized
    variable space.)

[+] This is why the st_nlink field of a directory ('man stat')
    is almost always two plus the number of directories within, because there
    are always two entries ('.', and the actual name of the directory
    that's within '..'), plus the '..' of all subdirectories, which
    must point back to the parent.  The 'find' program can use this
    information to count the number of subdirectories within a
    directory, as well -- though certain implementations of some
    file systems (I think fat, for one, in older kernels) have
    broken this assumption in the past.

