Se�n � Donnchadha <sean@forget.about.it> wrote:

> Not quite. The linker, given "foo", finds "foo.sa", which is simply
> the import library for a particular major revision of foo.

Depends.  With Linux a.out (and allegedly SunOS -- that was before my
time), this was true.  With ELF, no it ain't.  Here's how it *actually*
works.

You give `-lfoo' as an option to `cc' when you link.  `cc' invokes `ld'
passing it `-lfoo' and a pile of other arguments.

`ld' sees `-lfoo' and tries to find one of `libfoo.so' (shared library,
preferable) or `libfoo.a' (static library, less preferable) in certain
standard places.  Let's say it finds `/usr/lib/libfoo.so'.

Now that's actually a symbolic link to `libfoo.so.5.2'[1].  The linker
reads the symbol table of the library and resolves references in the
executable it's building.  It also reads the soname of the library and
sticks it in the program's header; the soname is `libfoo.so.5'.

When the program runs, the dynamic loader digs through the program's
header and tries to find all the libraries it refers to.  It finds
`libfoo.so.5' and looks in its various directories (specified in the
program header, and in the LD_LIBRARY_PATH variable) for a file of this
name, and finds one in `/usr/lib'.  It again is a symlink to
`libfoo.so.5.2'.  The library is mapped and symbols are resolved.

There aren't any of these bogus `.sa' files lying around.  The bad old
days of a.out are far behind us.

[1] Usually.  Sometimes the actual library is called something more
    bizarre like `libfoo-5.2.so', or the `.so' file is a linker script.
    Let's ignore these things for now and concentrate on the simple
    stuff.

-- [mdw]



