something about:
> seebs@plethora.net (Peter Seebach) wrote:
> >>What difference? That two hard links to the same file are considered
> >>the same library? Oh yeah, that's just *SO* profound.
> >
> >It is, actually.  If you can't see why, well, that's why you're using
> >Windows.  :)
> >
> >(Hint:  'foo.2.so' and 'foo.2.x.so' will be the same file for some changable
> >x.)
> >
> 
> Sounds just like how "MFC42.DLL version x" can be the same file for
> changeable values of x.

Err... what I believe Peter is trying to say is the profound implication of
everything being referenced by the inode, rather than any sort of 
"versioning" you place on top of it (please note the quotes). The inode is 
THE reference point for a file, so the name can be whatever you wish 
(including any naming convention you can think of), but the linker still 
points to the same file because the inode hasn't changed. Don't forget that
all of this is in the filesystem so you don't have to worry about any
internal details of the library (if you wish).

This mechanism is used on other platforms as well (for example, the OpenVMS
queue manager uses this for referencing files).

> Next time, before you turn on the smugness, you may want to make sure
> you understand what's being discussed.

