sean@forget.about.it (Se�n � Donnchadha) writes:

> But this *DOESN'T* avoid incompatibility! When you install a minor
> library revision, programs using the previous one get switched over to
> use the new one. *THAT'S* where incompatibility can creep in, and Unix
> can do nothing about that.

And nor can anything else; if you change the library incompatibly
without changing the name used by the runtime linker (i.e. the soname)
to distinguish between versions then you are going to be in trouble.
But in what way is that different from just having a buggy version of
the library?  Clearly the author intends instances of the library with
the same soname to implement the same interface.

You could address this by e.g. using md5 of the library code as the
soname - but in reality the problem doesn't seem to be so large that
such extreme measures are necessary.  Certainly can't recall having
encountered any version skew problems in shared library in any of the
unixen I use.

