sean@forget.about.it (Se�n � Donnchadha) wrote:

[snip]

>No offense, but what you're saying here is nonsense. If your makefile
>specifies "libfoo", the linker will use "libfoo"; it won't assume that
>you're using this or that naming convention and look for a file with
>some other name. The name of the shared library file may be abstracted
>away elegantly - with things like symbolic links, import libraries, or
>pragmas - but making development tools depend on a naming convention
>is gross.

The linker links against libfoo.so which is a symlink to libfoo.so.1.0.3.
However libfoo.so.1.0.3 has an internal name libfoo.so.1.0, which the
linker puts into the created executable. Thus when the application is
run it loads libfoo.so.1.0 (which may be a symlink to libfoo.so.1.0.5)
rather than against libfoo.so (which by now is a symlink to libfoo.so.2.0.0).

Norman



