In article <37e49171.450690508@news.shore.net>,
Se�n � Donnchadha <sean@forget.about.it> wrote:
>Not quite. The linker, given "foo", finds "foo.sa", which is simply
>the import library for a particular major revision of foo.

Roughly, yes.  But it can figure out which one it wants.

>>After this, the program knows which version it requires, and which other
>>versions it may be okay with.

>What the heck does "may be okay with" mean? The program certainly does
>know which version it requires - say 1.2.3. Now, how does it know
>whether it will work with 1.2.4 when that's released?

Two options:

1.  The developer just said "1.2", in which case, it assumes it works with
1.2.4.  Since the sytem provided a mechanism for library maintainers to
indicate level of compatability, it is almost certainly right.
2.  The developer said "1.2.3", in which case, it ignores a newer version.

>Answer: it
>doesn't. When 1.2.4 is installed, the program will start using it - a
>combination that may not have been lab-tested.

If the developer cares, the developer will have specified "1.2.3" as the
requested version.

>>	1.  All versions, including old ones, of a library, so you can
>>	avoid incompatability.

>But this *DOESN'T* avoid incompatibility! When you install a minor
>library revision, programs using the previous one get switched over to
>use the new one. *THAT'S* where incompatibility can creep in, and Unix
>can do nothing about that.

Except that, if the programs are worried, they bind to the old version
anyway.

Unix gives you a choice.

More importantly, because it is *standard and easy* to indicate mild
version changes, and distinguish them from major ones, the people producing
the libraries actually do so.

>Great, now if only I knew what "foo" meant. Admit it, a standard
>mechanism for tagging an executable with descriptive text of arbitrary
>length is a whole lot better. And Windows provides that.

I am unlikely to admit something so godawful stupid.  Descriptive text is
cute, but not useful.  You can't have a program parse the text, and developers
in Windows-land pick descriptive text that you can't actually read - because
they use different conventions.

A standard convention is a very good thing.  It's like which side of the road
you drive on; the important thing is that people agree.

-s
