In article <6wwD3.62$Fh.7932@typhoon-sf.snfc21.pbi.net>,
Chad Mulligan <cmulligan@hipcrime.vocab.org> wrote:
>No it doesn't.  If you feel your skills are greater than those writers and
>editors, don't use them.  I've found that while I don't neccessarily agree
>with the findings of a grammar checker, the results are valid.

I have found that grammar checkers are often stupid.

You have mistaken programmers for their programs.  A grammar checker trying to
enforce the rules that someone has tried to write down is not the same as the
grammarian who was consulted about those rules.

I've written programs which assist in debugging other code.  They give advice.
The advice is sometimes wrong.  My advice is much better, even though I wrote
the programs.

In particular, the Word grammar checker is famous for
	Sir or Madam Winston Churchill, when he or she was a boy or girl...

which isn't grammar, it's just stupid.

>No, that is grammar.  At least that's what my sophmore english teacher, a
>widely published author told me.  If you would like other examples read
>Dickens, or R. L. Stephenson. You find short paragraphs of simple sentences.

You are either stupid or trolling.  The fact that a given trait is often seen
in grammatical work does not make it a feature of grammar.

Clear writing is good style; that does not mean that unclear writing is
ungrammatical.

A style checker and a grammar checker are approaching different tasks.

"John's friend Mary gave John's friend Mary's umbrella to John's friend Mary's
friend Jane."  Grammatical, but bad style.  A grammar checker, per se, has no
reason to flag this as being in error; I would certainly hope that a style
checker would catch it.

Lint is not a compiler.

