In article <7rjl08$efp$1@ben.hks.net>, Doug DeJulio <ddj@hks.net> wrote:
>Existing programs that specify to use library versions 1 or 1.2 will
>switch to using version 1.2.3.  I believe that any program that
>specified version 1.2.2 specifically at link time (note: doing this
>can be tricky) should continue using version 1.2.2.
>
>I'd say this is exactly the correct behavior.

The correct behavior would be to try to load the exact version that
was used at link time; if that wasn't available, remove one level
of versioning and try again (so if 1.2.2 existed, it would be used;
else it would try 1.2 (which would be a symbolic link to 1.2.3); else
it would simply try 1).  Although there are linkers that do this, the
Linux ld.so isn't one of them.

There are a lot of different naming schemes combined with symbolic links
and sonames that make sense. The one I favor for Linux libraries is to
make the soname be completely specific; if you want to upgrade libraries,
replace it with a symbolic link to the newer version (e.g. symbolic link
from 1.2.2 to 1.2, which is a symbolic link to 1.2.3). That gives you
complete control over whether old programs use the old or new version
of a library.

Unfortunately, that's pretty global. To override the library choice for
just one application, you need to wrap it in something that will set the
path for the loader so it finds the correct version of the library first.



