Se�n � Donnchadha <sean@forget.about.it> wrote:
> mdw@catbert.ebi.ac.uk (Mark Wooding) wrote:
> 
> >> 32-bit DLLs use full path names, just like Unix.
> >
> >Factually wrong.  Unix distinguishes shared libraries by inode, not
> >pathname.  The difference is obvious and profound;
> 
> What difference? That two hard links to the same file are considered
> the same library? Oh yeah, that's just *SO* profound.

Also consider that two files with the same pathname can have different
inodes (at different times).  And that inodes don't have to have
pathnames at all.

The important point here is that *upgrading* a Unix system's libraries
is trivial.  The *only* part of any of my Linux systems that I have to
reboot after upgrading is the kernel (and even then, I can add and
remove kernel modules as I go).  I can upgrade Debian 1.3 to Debian 2.1
*without rebooting*.  That's a change of almost all the tools, and a
major-version upgrade of the C library.  And the damned thing just
carries on working.

Windows doesn't seem to have a concept analogous to Unix's inodes.  (If
it does, it doesn't seem to show much.)  You can't delete or replace a
shared library while it's in use, which is sort of a shame because you
then need to fiddle with the file before the system starts up properly.
Not pretty, or clever.

Hard links are almost an irrelevance.

-- [mdw]



