I use grammar checkers for comic relief only.

(Oh, change that first comma to a colon, and replace the full stop with
a semicolon or dash.)

[Word's grammar checker has difficulty with competently written
English.]

> No, it has trouble with technically written English, Gramatik
> acknowledged this in their v5 for DOS release notes, IIRC. Well
> looking at your writings here, you have the same grammatical
> difficulty I have in correspondence: Sentences that are too long.  It
> is a common failing of technicians who write.  Standard grammar
> defines sentence as a single idea.  We tend to put multiple concepts
> in a single complex sentence.  A paragraph would more properly serve
> in most cases.

I write long sentences because I like to, because I love the sound of my
language in free flow.  When writing technically, I use short sentences
and short paragraphs; when writing for fun, I let myself off the leash
and enjoy myself.

It's evident that the quality of written English has declined over the
last hundred years.  Where are the rich sentence structures of the last
century?

I'd like to compare two pieces of text.  I'll steal one from the quoted
block just above here:

  Exhibit A: `No, it has trouble with technically written English,
  Gramatik acknowledged this in their v5 for DOS release notes, IIRC'

A longish sentence.  Probably too long: the second comma ought to be a
full stop.

  Exhibit B: `No: it has trouble with technically written English, as I
  believe Gramatik acknowledged in their v5 for DOS release notes.'

It's very slightly longer, and only subtly different, but it has better
flow.  I'd be happy with leaving that as a single sentence.  Indeed, I
prefer Exhibit B over a two-sentence version, although the authors of
Writing for Drooling Imbeciles would rap me sharply over the knuckles
for it.

[Windows programs are alleged to be `intuitive'; user errors or user
interface bugs?]

> I always make allowances for eunuchs users too.  As these eunuchs always
> want to blame software for their own shortcomings.

Silly (but true) story: My Most Embarassing Unix Accident

I was building a program which was configured with Autoconf.  I think it
was one I'd written.  I wanted it to install into my home directory, so
I typed this:

  $ ./configure --prefix=~
  $ make
  $ make install

It didn't work.  I looked, and Autoconf had installed the software in a
directory called `~', just as I'd told it to.  `That's wrong,' I
muttered to myself, `I don't want the software there.'  So I typed

  $ rm -rf ~

This was all my fault, for not having completely understood which
programs expanded `~' sequences and under which circumstances.

I'm quite happy to accept my shortcomings with regard to software which
expects an intelligent and educated user; Windows programs, as
advertised, don't expect that, so I blame the programs instead.

-- [mdw]



