glmar04@twirl.mcc.ac.uk (Geoff Lane) wrote:

>>
>> Please describe the scheme you're envisioning. A word of warning
>> though: I have put much thought into the shared library problem and
>> will be happy to demonstrate that your scheme is full of holes.
>
>As implemented in most modern versions of Unix.  You can specify PER-PROCESS
>the search path for libraries (static and shared) If you need to override
>system library functionallity you just arrange for the overriding code to be
>seen first by the link loader.  Of course, it's up to the app creator to
>arrange this.  The new functionality is held in a library file somewhere
>within the app's installation directory. Nothing gets overwritten.  Only
>the app sees the new functionallity.
>

You can do this in Windows as well, and there are several ways to set
it up, the easiest of which is simply to install all libraries into
the directory holding the application executable (the loader always
looks there first). But this effectively amounts to static linking.
You're giving up the disk/memory advantages of shared libraries, as
well as their field-upgradability.



