Raymond Wiker <raymond@orion.no> wrote:

>> 
>> That's great, but it's a totally unrelated issue. Here's the real
>> question: What is the installer to do when a new program requires
>> libfoo-1.2.3, and libfoo-1.2.2 is already installed and in use by
>> dozens of other programs?
>
>        Simple. You copy libfoo-1.2.3 to the appropriate place, where
>it will peacefully coexist with libfoo-1.2.2. The file names are not
>identical, so they *can* be in the same directory.
>

It doesn't matter whether the files can coexist. As long as apps use
only one of them, the other one may as well not be there.

>
>        The installation will (probably(???)) also update a symlink for
>libfoo-1.2, so that it points to libfoo-1.2.3 instead of
>libfoo-1.2.2. *No* programs should break from this, as there should be 
>no visible differences between 1.2.2 and 1.2.3 - if there were, the
>new version would be called 1.3.0, or something.
>

You're not seeing the real problem, Raymond. What you're saying (that
library authors simply bump the major version number if the library
introduces incompatible changes) is only true when the authors
actually realize that they've made such changes. Shared library
conflicts happen when the authors *DON'T* realize it, usually because
they didn't have the ability to test their updated library against
every app that uses it. Because they don't realize that a
compatibility glitch exists, they issue a minor update instead of a
major one, and things blow up.

And this isn't even always the library authors' fault. No matter how
well-written the documentation, there is always room for
interpretation when it comes to software behavior. A library update
may actually be 100% back-compatible in terms of the authors' own
understanding of the required behavior, while app authors may have
their own ideas - ones that lead to their apps depending on behavior
that the library author would consider incorrect or unspecified.

>
>I suspect that an
>application would also be able to request a particular minor version,
>as well.
>

No. This would eliminate the field-ugradability of shared libraries.

>
>        Compare this with DLLs. There are several versions of
>MFC42.DLL, and they all expect to reside in the same directory, with
>the same name. There is *no* major/minor version information
>available, although you can kludge this by using different file names
>for different major numbers.
>

Incorrect. In Windows, the major version number is in the filename,
and the minor version number is in the VERSIONINFO resource embedded
into the file. At app installation time, this resource is compared
against the one in the file already installed to determine whether an
update should occur. But instead of installing multiple files and
using a link to select one of them, Windows simply installs only one.
The end result is the same - apps use the latest version installed.
The Unix approach has the advantage that a library update is easy to
back out - you just revert the link instead of having to reinstall the
original library from the distribution media. But this is something
the average user wouldn't have the ability to take advantage of, and
it has the (minor) disadvantage of wasting disk space in the general
case.

>
>In practice, this is seldom
>done. MFC42.DLL may appear to have a major version encoded in the
>filename, but that is not the case - there have been versions of this
>DLL that has been incompatible with programs written for earlier
>versions. There are other cases like this.
>

Right, but this is precisely what I was talking about before. The
library authors (Microsoft in this case) believed that their MFC42
update was 100% back-compatible, so they didn't change the major
version number (by renaming the library to MFC43 or something). Had
they realized that compatibility glitches existed, they would have
renamed the library and everything would have been peachy keen. As I
hope I've convinced you, the Unix approach wouldn't have helped at
all.

>> 
>> You must not have been around in the early days when HJ Lu included a
>> list of known broken programs with each libc revision.
>
>        So? The state of the art in Linux libraries is improving,
>which is *not* something you can say about Microsoft libraries. 
>

Huh? Would you care to elaborate?

>>
>> This problem really has little to do with versioning. The whole point
>> of shared libraries (aside from disk/memory usage) is independent
>> field-ugradability, which results in the runtime coupling of two
>> binary components that may not have been lab-tested in combination. In
>> this particular case, there's no way to eliminate the risk without
>> also eliminating the benefit.
>
>        You *can* get around this, as long as it is possible for the
>application to request a particular version (major version, minor
>version, directory...)
>

Again, what you're suggesting would sacrifice the major advantage of
shared libraries - field-upgradability.



