On 13 Sep 1999 16:44:53 GMT, "Joseph T. Adams" <joe@apk.net> wrote:

>:
>: You can do this in Windows as well, and there are several ways to set
>: it up, the easiest of which is simply to install all libraries into
>: the directory holding the application executable (the loader always
>: looks there first). But this effectively amounts to static linking.
>: You're giving up the disk/memory advantages of shared libraries, as
>: well as their field-upgradability.
>
>
>Umm, what happens if a same-named, yet incompatible, version of the
>same DLL (or OCX) is already loaded in memory?
>
>I was always under the impression that you can't have multiple
>same-named DLLs in memory in Windows at the same time.
>

Windows considers all 16-bit DLLs with the same module name (file
name) to be the same. 32-bit DLLs use full path names, just like Unix.
So to be perfectly precise, the behavior depends on the bit-ness of
the DLL in question. I was going under the assumption that this
discussion was limited to 32-bit DLLs.



