Paul Lutus wrote:

> Not at all. Unless there is an implied dependent clause, "Than me" is
> the
> correct form. The "Than I" form is a frequently-derided error.

Actually, Paul, in English the word _than_ always involves an implied
clause.  "You are a better C programmer than I" is the same as "You are
a better C programmer than I [am]."  When you put in the implied parts
of the sentence, it becomes obvious that the subjective case (_I_) is
required, not the objective case (_me_).

_The modern writer's handbook_, 2nd ed. (O'Hare) points this out on p.
219:

    In an elliptical, or incomplete, clause, place the pronoun in the
