The last problem I had (of quite a few) was that the system just rebooted
itself right around the time it started up the network (sometimes before
starting the "lo" device, sometimes after). I had also had the machine lock
up at the end of my first attempt installing RH 6.0 (after the Xconfigurator
asked me if I wanted to boot into X mode, I chose "no", then machine was
dead). After wiping the disk and installing again, I succeeded in getting
through the complete install, reboot, and compiled a new kernel to install
with one more time. Once I finally got everything installed the way I
wanted, I applied all the latest errata updates, started configuring my
network interfaces and the machine started it's rebooting... I then gave up
since I was running out of time and switched back to NT.

Other problems I encountered were:

1) The kernel shipped with RH6.0 (based on 2.2.5) contains a QLogic ISP
driver with a bug that keeps it from properly recognizing drives over 8GB.
Since I was installing onto a 45G hardware RAID array, this was a bit of a
problem. This bug was corrected in 2.2.11.

2) The need to use SRM/aboot for booting means two things: the selection of
SCSI host adapters you can boot from is very limited (so I couldn't use my
Adaptec 2904UW any more), and the boot disk must be BSD-disklabeled (all
other disks can use DOS-style disklabels). BSD disklabels are limited to
1024 cylinders, which again limits you to a boot disk under 8GB.


