20:36:33 GMT, seebs@plethora.net (Peter Seebach) wrote:

>>>
>>>It sure would be nice, though, if the person called in to fix the box
>>>had access to diagnostic tools.
>
>>What tools are you thinking of, that aren't available in Windows?
>
>A convenient way to see what versions of which files are available, and
>spare copies of the old ones?
>

You can use Explorer to see what's installed, and the context menu or
File->Properties to inspect version info. You can use SFC to see which
libraries have been modified since the last known good state. You can
use MSINFO to get a list of all loaded modules with their version
information. There are *LOTS* of tools available, although for some
you'd have to go to the Resource Kit or a third-party product.

>>
>>Sure they do. MFC is just one example.
>
>They don't - GDI32 is another example.
>

No, GDI32 isn't an example of anything. Remember, we agreed earlier
that the name of the library doesn't have to change if the update is
100% back-compatible. Such is the case with GDI32; it's authors
believe (mistakenly or not) that each release of GDI32 has been
back-compatible with all previous releases. Even if you have evidence
to the contrary - that a GDI32 update broke something - that doesn't
prove that Microsoft knowingly issued an incompatible update.

>>
>>Huh? A naming convention makes it *EASIER* to rename something? That's
>>news to me.
>
>A naming convention means the new name is predictable.  You say "the first
>version of the library will be 1.0, the second would be 2.0".  Now, you don't
>have to come up with 3-letter prefixes to leave enough space for a real
>version.
>

For a long time now Windows has had long filename support, so you
don't have to worry about "leaving enough space" for anything. Aside
from that, I see no argument in what you're saying. Neither Unix nor
Windows enforces any kind of naming convention, so I fail to see how
Unix is better here.

>
>I give up.  You'll always believe it's reasonable that a Windows machine
>which runs "SmartUpdate" and on which someone installs two or three Microsoft
>applications needs to be reinstalled after a while.
>

I'd appreciate it if you didn't tell me what I think is reasonable. In
fact, I think it's *TOTALLY* unreasonable that people like you
reinstall when most of the time it really isn't necessary, blaming
Windows instead of your own laziness in properly diagnosing whatever
problem you're having. Somehow I think you'd give Unix or whatever
your pet OS is much more of a chance.

>
>I'll always think it's
>reasonable that I can switch executable formats on a machine without a
>reinstall.
>

Is that supposed to be impressive or something?



