>That's a good point, but I submit that most users (the kind of folks
>that don't know a shared library from a public library) wouldn't be
>able to take advantage of that. Here, again, Unix benefits from its
>computer-literate user base.

No, it's the other way around: the computer-literate user base benefits
from Unix.  It may be that in this respect, Unix and Windows are going
to be equally difficult for the naive user.  But the point is that
Windows is almost as difficult for the computer-literate user, whereas
Unix is much easier for the computer-literate user.

>>>Only if the authors realize that the library change breaks existing
>>>apps. Conflicts happen when the authors *DON'T* realize it, and
>>>neither Unix's nor Windows' scheme can protect against that.
>>
>>The difference is, if the author *does* know, Windows *still* doesn't
>>help.
>
>No help is required. If the authors of a Windows shared library know
>that an update is not back-compatible, they can do exactly what they'd
>do under Unix - give the updated library a new name (e.g. MFC30 ->
>MFC40). That works just as well in Windows as it does in Unix.

What about the case when the authors of the program (not the authors
of the shared library) know that the program is not compatible with
a supposedly minor update of the library?
Under Linux, the program's author can quite easily tell the linker to
ensure their program will only link with the compatible version
of the library.  Under Windows, that's not possible, AFAIK.
Indeed after an upgrade of the library (e.g. when installing some other
app), the compatible version won't even be on the disk anymore, since
it will have been overwritten by the incompatible version.
So even if the program could detect the situation, there's not much it
could do about it, except to tell the user that they need to reinstall...

