sean@forget.about.it (Sean Donnchadha) writes:

>seebs@plethora.net (Peter Seebach) wrote:
>
>>>
>>>That's great, but it's a totally unrelated issue. Here's the real
>>>question: What is the installer to do when a new program requires
>>>libfoo-1.2.3, and libfoo-1.2.2 is already installed and in use by
>>>dozens of other programs?
>>
>>Installs it also, because the files will just sit next to each other.
>
>See below.
>
>>In that case, the existing programs will probably switch to using 1.2.3.
>
>Then why keep 1.2.2 around?

One reason is because probably != definitely.
In Linux, it's possible for the program authors to have explicitly linked
their programs against 1.2.2, in which case they will continue to use 1.2.2
rather than using 1.2.3.  This technique is a useful tool to enable
program authors to avoid known incompatibilities.  It doesn't avoid the
problem of unknown incompatibilities but it does give program authors an
easy way to avoid such incompatibilities once they become known.

