On 14 Sep 1999 08:47:08 GMT, fjh@cs.mu.oz.au (Fergus Henderson) wrote:

>>
>>That's a good point, but I submit that most users (the kind of folks
>>that don't know a shared library from a public library) wouldn't be
>>able to take advantage of that. Here, again, Unix benefits from its
>>computer-literate user base.
>
>No, it's the other way around: the computer-literate user base benefits
>from Unix.
>

I disagree. Unix benefits because it doesn't have to open itself up to
the naive user to the degree that Windows does. Because Unix systems
are maintained by people who know what they're doing, they're on
average more properly configured and therefore enjoy a reputation of
superior reliability. In reality, it's not exactly a fair comparison,
and it gets even less fair when you take into account the amount of
new software (both system and application) installed on an average
Windows box per unit time, the number of supported hardware
configurations, and the fact that most Windows device drivers are
hacked together under the ridiculous time-to-market pressures of IHVs
locked in frantic competition and willing to take every shortcut
necessary to win the inevitable magazine performance comparisons.

>
>It may be that in this respect, Unix and Windows are going
>to be equally difficult for the naive user.  But the point is that
>Windows is almost as difficult for the computer-literate user, whereas
>Unix is much easier for the computer-literate user.
>

I disagree here as well. Unix and Windows are in no way equally
difficult for the naive user. Windows can certainly be problematic,
but it's accessible. To the average user, Unix is still completely
impenetrable.

>
>What about the case when the authors of the program (not the authors
>of the shared library) know that the program is not compatible with
>a supposedly minor update of the library?
>Under Linux, the program's author can quite easily tell the linker to
>ensure their program will only link with the compatible version
>of the library.  Under Windows, that's not possible, AFAIK.
>

Come on now. The app author can simply install the required library
version in the directory holding the app's main executable.



