On 14 Sep 1999 09:42:59 GMT, fjh@cs.mu.oz.au (Fergus Henderson) wrote:

>>
>>Huh? A naming convention makes it *EASIER* to rename something? That's
>>news to me.
>
>Actually yes, a naming convention *does* make it easier.
>
>If the next update of libfoo-1 is not binary compatible, but remains
>source compatible, then the developers of libfoo can call the new version
>libfoo-2, and the developers of programs that use libfoo need only
>recompile, without changing any of their source code or makefiles, since
>their makefiles refer only to `libfoo', not to `libfoo-1'.  In contrast,
>if you don't have a naming convention, the makefiles (or project files
>or whatever equivalent you have) for programs that use libfoo must
>refer to `libfoo-1', rather than `libfoo', and so they must be modified
>whenever the major version number changes.
>

No offense, but what you're saying here is nonsense. If your makefile
specifies "libfoo", the linker will use "libfoo"; it won't assume that
you're using this or that naming convention and look for a file with
some other name. The name of the shared library file may be abstracted
away elegantly - with things like symbolic links, import libraries, or
pragmas - but making development tools depend on a naming convention
is gross.



