>>>can include a
>>> README saying "This program requires libfoo-1.2.3" and 
>>>leave it up to
>>> the user to figure out how to set that up without 
>>>killing another
>>> program that requires libfoo-1.2.2. Windows apps have 
>>>no such option;
>>> they *MUST* install the libraries they need without the 
>>>user's help.

>>Nonsense - any modern Linux distribution checks for 
>>dependencies, and
>>there are very nice tools that automatically installs any 
>>required stuff along with the application.

>That's great, but it's a totally unrelated issue. Here's 
>the real
>question: What is the installer to do when a new program 
>requires
>libfoo-1.2.3, and libfoo-1.2.2 is already installed and in 
>use by dozens of other programs?

>>Without breaking any existing applications, one might add.

>You must not have been around in the early days when HJ Lu 
>included a
>list of known broken programs with each libc revision.

>This problem really has little to do with versioning. The 
>whole point
>of shared libraries (aside from disk/memory usage) is 
>independent
>field-ugradability, which results in the runtime coupling 
>of two
>binary components that may not have been lab-tested in 
>combination. In
>this particular case, there's no way to eliminate the risk 
>without also eliminating the benefit.

The main benefit being - if libfoo-1.2.2 has a known bug, 
and libfoo-1.2.3 fixes it, you get a bunch of applications 
fixed for free. If you force all the old applications to 
carry on using libfoo-1.2.2, you prevent that bug from 
being fixed.

On Windows, you can run ten year old programs for Windows 
3.0 and they look like nice new Windows 98 applications. 
This is a valuable use of libraries. DLL hell would not 
exist if there weren't significant benefits.

Versioning is an important part of the library problem, but 
it isn't a total solution. 

J (Looking Backward)

