You're misinterpreting your observations. It may very well be that in
your experience, no other programs have broken. But you're taking that
observation and drawing the totally invalid conclusion that Unix has
somehow solved the problem. Well, not even close. Just because you've
never seen someone get shot, doesn't mean that people don't get shot.

One thing you have to remember is that Unix made very little use of
shared libraries until very recently. When SVR3 was king for example,
Unix shared libraries were incredibly difficult to create, and
programs were limited to something like three or four shared
libraries.

>
>Maybe one can do equivilent things in windows as in Unix.  But even if
>it can be done, it *isn't* being done on a regular basis.  As an
>informed a mostly competent user of Windows, I get very annoyed when
>I'm forced to track down errors that could have been prevented by 
>the OS recognising and allowing the use of multiple versions of 
>shared libraries.
>

Since Windows *DOES* recognize and allow the use of multiple versions
of shared libraries, it is obvious that this capability is *NOT*
sufficient for preventing the problem.



