In article <37df4efc.433677244@news.shore.net>,
Se�n � Donnchadha <sean@forget.about.it> wrote:
>No offense, but what you're saying here is nonsense. If your makefile
>specifies "libfoo", the linker will use "libfoo"; it won't assume that
>you're using this or that naming convention and look for a file with
>some other name.

You don't understand.

'libfoo' *IS* 'libfoo.2.0.so'.

The linker, given "foo", looks for the most-recent version of "libfoo.*.so",
and writes into the program it creates which version it found.

After this, the program knows which version it requires, and which other
versions it may be okay with.

>The name of the shared library file may be abstracted
>away elegantly - with things like symbolic links, import libraries, or
>pragmas - but making development tools depend on a naming convention
>is gross.

Making development tools use a naming convention allows you to avoid DLL
hell.  It lets you have two vital things:
	1.  All versions, including old ones, of a library, so you can
	avoid incompatability.
	2.  A reliable way to deterministically find any library which
	implements the API for "foo".

