> One possible, but ugly, solution, might be the Dynamic Matching
> approach. Every call is encoded into a packet, with arguments
> that can be type-checked (e.g., they are derived from a common
> base class and passed in as pointers or something); the callee
> would be responsible for checking the arguments, matching
> them against what it wants the arguments to be (supplying
> defaults as required), and ultimately accepting the packet
> and doing something reasonable with it, or rejecting it and throwing
> an exception. As a software package evolves, the callee arguments
> will change, but no callee or callee argument is ever deleted.
> (It's ugly because it will yield dead code after awhile, and the
> current C++ syntax can't handle defaults within the middle
> of a call. Memory mangement might be a bit of a nightmare.)
