"Daniel Johnson" <daniel.n.johnson@worldnet.att.net> writes:

>"File names that contain the version number" is what Windows does; that's
>what the the 42 is in mfc42.dll, and what the 32 is in gdi32.dll.

This is only half correct; the '42' in 'mfc42.dll' does indeed signify
that this library implements the Microsoft Foundation Classes version 4.2,
but the '32' in 'gdi32.dll' simply says that this library implements
the *32-bit* GDI routines (as opposed to the 16-bit GDI routines, which
are implemented by 'gdi.exe').  This has nothing to do with versioning,
as 32- vs. 16-bit is not a question of version: you always need *both*
halves of the 32-/16-bit DLL pair, and in fact you need the same *version*
of both halves for anything to work, as the two call into each other
(at least on Windows 9x).

The *version* of gdi32.dll is not reflected in the file name; indeed,
there are a lot of different versions (from Win95 Rev. A/B/C, Win98,
Win98 SE, various versions of Win NT ...).  Even more problematic is
the case of, say, comctl32.dll, because there you don't just get a new
version with each new release of the OS, but even *applications*,
most notably Internet Explorer, install new versions of this essential
system DLL ...

In fact, none of the Windows system DLLs proper contain a version number
in their name.  The only MS DLLs I'm aware of that do this are the 
C runtime libraries (MSVC*.DLL, MFC*.DLL), but these are not included
in the base OS IIRC; you just get them if you install any application
compiled with a MS C compiler ...

