Just as a side note.  Microsoft now claims that they are selling
100,000 copies of Windows NT per month.  Linux vendors are openly
admitting to doing that much or more, per week.  Red Hat sold 2 million
copies last year, and claims growth of 300% (10%/month).  SuSE claims
500% over last year, but only sold 1 million copies.  Caldera hasn't
given numbers yet.

Included in those numbers is VA Systems (formerly VA Research) which
is doing 240% growth per QUARTER.

Part of this is because Linux is now going after the Windows 95
desktop market.  As more applications are coming out for Linux,
and more OEMs get behind Linux, it looks like Linux is going to
get very popular very quickly.  Given that most vendors will
want to beat Mandrake, it's quite likely that Linux will be
getting very friendly.

I hope the OEMs will start providing support for USB and DVDs, even
if only in a commercial format ($$).  I understand the issues with
DVDs, and it's unlikely that the Motion Picture industry and the
Music industry will want to open the first good copyright protection
