rmk@toad.rmkhome.com writes:

> David Wragg <dpw@doc.ic.ac.uk> wrote:
> 
> :Tru64 is not a BSD. Ultrix, DEC's original Unix, was a BSD. Tru64,
> :AKA Digital Unix, AKA OSF/1, is a SVR4 derivative, IIRC.
> 
> Well, OSF/1 was originally based on SVR2 plus BSD 4.3/4.4.
> 
> Fun fact. OSF had loads of Intel 486 boxes running OSF1 at one timw.

Why is this surprising?  The x86 box was one of the three reference platforms
(the others being the DEC Pmax and the Encore 32532 with 16 processors).  Just
because DEC, Hitachi and the IBM mainframe group shipped OSF/1 on their
systems, it doesn't mean that they were reference platforms.

Originally everybody had Pmaxen on their desks, and the encores in the lab, but
over the years it generally changed to 486's and pentiums, with the encores
still used for aggressive n-way SMP testing.  Besides being cheap, the x86 was
the one platform that didn't favor one vendor over an other unnecessarily (not
that it really matter, since at their heart of hearts, the sponsor companies
wanted a magic silver bullet to be UNIX compatible, but didn't want to have to
change their code base to get that bullet).

We did have a horrid time getting OSF/1 stable on x86's at first.  Part of the
stability problems were traced to the way the x86 REP MOVS instruction works.
There is a bit as to whether to move forwards or backwards, and neither the
compiler nor the kernel ever set this bit at the time (but it turns out the X
server and/or the library does set it).  I was running a mousetrapped kernel to
test more validations, and it went off just before the party for the first
release.  I grabbed a kernel engineer and we were looking at it, and determined
it was a memory move that was causing read-only pages to be modified (and later
flushed to disk).  Having read the x86 manual the night before, I asked whats
the status of the memory move bit, and he says "what memory move bit".  While
it is easy to blame the kernel guys, I did have to fix GCC in at least two
places for the exact same thing.


