Vivek Venugopalan (vivek@mcs.donotusethis.net) wrote:
: 	Can I add a PCI modem to an Alphastation AS/200?

Depends which one.

: I have heard
: that PCI modems are memory mapped and so its not possible.  Is that
: true?

No, this is not true.  The problem is that __most__ PCI modems on a
market are "winmodems", i.e. they implement DSP and UART functions of a
modem in a software library with upublished specs and supplied by
manufacturers only for Windows.  That way they save a few dollars
(literally) by unloading hardware functions on your CPU which now have
to service a phone line in "real-time" (the last requirement is a big
trouble even on Windows which "own" hardware as people trying to play
games over a modem found really fast).

There are PCI modems which are real modems.  See, for example,
www.actiontec.com.  These will work with Linux just fine.  Also __some__
PCI modems from USR are not of "loose" kind.  It is often very hard to
find on a packaging, or even in "documentation", with what you are
dealing and many people were bitten.

For more of the story www.linmodems.org

  Michal



