Ketil Z Malde <ketil@ii.uib.no> wrote:

>>
>> I agree with him. DLL Hell is a popular scapegoat whenever someone is
>> too lazy to put effort into diagnosing the problem. DLL conflicts do
>> happen, but much less frequently than the FUDsters would have you
>> believe. 
>
>Okay, so the reason MSDS crashes if I click on a DSW file with the
>latest service pack installed is...?  As usual, there is no
>debugging information available that could help me solve the problem,
>but just to show you that it's not just laziness, if you tell me how
>to properly diagnose and fix it, I'll reinstall the SP again.
>

I assume that by "MSDS" you mean "MS DevStudio", based on your mention
of DSW (DevStudio Workspace) files. What makes you think "DLL Hell" is
to blame? Which DLLs are involved?

It seems to me that a bug in MSDS or a bug in the OS update could
cause the crash you're describing, and it doesn't have to have
anything to do with DLLs.

>>
>> And neither Linux nor any other OS has a real solution.
>
>Of course Linux (and Unix in general) has
>
>* applications that don't delete system components on installation
>

Linux has the luxury of a computer-literate user base. It's standard
practice for Linux apps to include READMEs saying, "By the way, this
app requires libfoo-1.2.3 or higher". In the Windows world this isn't
an option; apps must ship with all the libraries they need (relative
to the OS distribution, of course) and install everything with minimal
help from the user.

>*
>* versioned shared libraries, so applications get exactly what they expect
>

Please. Unix's notion of "versioned shared libraries" is a version
number placed into the filename. Well, Windows can do that too, and
often does (MFC.DLL -> MFC20.DLL -> MFC40.DLL -> MFC42.DLL, etc.). But
Windows also provides a standard mechanism for embedding version
resources in all executables (including DLLs), as well as version-
aware OS-level file installation APIs. In other words, Windows does
*MORE* to prevent DLL conflicts than Unix.

But conflicts can still happen. Both Windows and Unix allow DLLs to be
updated independently of the apps that use them. This is intentional
of course, as it leaves room for bug fixes in the DLL. But it also
leaves room for conflicts to creep in, and there's nothing either OS
can do about it without also sacrificing the convenience of DLLs.

>*
>* possibility of different sets of shared libraries available to
>* different applications 
>
>* possibility of preloading a particular library that would hide
>  system library functionality
>

Windows can do this as well. Apps can (and often do) install private
copies of DLLs in their directories. But again, this is a tradeoff.
You have to balance risk versus convenience and disk/memory usage. The
Unix situation is no different.

>
>so the equivalent of DLL conflict doesn't happen.
>

Quite the contrary.

>
>Windows DLLs are broken by design.
>

Nonsense.



