Exactly. It should not be possible to remove the library 
whan any of the applications using it are present in the 
system. It should be possible to overwrite it only with a 
newer version that supported the same interfaces. It should 
be possible for the applications using the library to 
access it independently of its location.

It certainly should not be the case that when another, 
totally seperate application looks for a file, it 
automatically looks in the same directory as the library 
because the directory is on a global, shared path. The 
library should not be stored in a system directory or 
general purpose applications directory with hundreds of 
other unconnected files.

I have heard of various Unix approaches to this problem, 
but I remain to be convinced that they aren't all kludges 
and workarounds. I would expect that a workable solution to 
the problem would apply to all libraries used by all 
applications.

J (Looking Backward)




