In article <37dc0c92.88995578@news.shore.net>, 
sean@forget.about.it (Se�n � Donnchadha) wrote:
..
>I'm afraid you don't understand the problem (no offense 
>intended). All
>library updates, no matter how minor, are incompatible. 
>The classic
>example is the app whose author accidentally made it 
>depend on buggy
>behavior in a library. The library author issues what he 
>believes to
>be a harmless and compatible bug fix, but ends up breaking 
>the app anyway.

This is an almost unavoidable issue. It would help if old 
versions of a library were retained, and that if an 
application failed to operate correctly with the new 
version, it could revert to using the old. When the new 
version of the library was installed, the user would be 
notified of the applications that would be affected and 
that would need testing.

It should also be possible to 'lock' an application to a 
particular library. It should also be possible (in the age 
of the Internet) for an application to obtain up-to-date 
information on what versions of libraries it supports.

Of course the situation is further complicated by the fact 
that libraries are often used by other libraries.

J (Looking Backward)


