	I can appreciate the point that you are making here. But this
is partly a problem with marketting. Windows went to a lot of effort
to be usuable by everyones granny. The problem is though that the
fundamental complexity of a computer is still there, and windows hasnt
hidden it all that well. It pops up regularly when you get DLL
hell or crashes for whatever reason. 

	The other part of the problem is of course the idea that
computers are inherantly a good thing. They have been introduced into
many many areas of our life where they do not actually do any good,
and in fact often confuse what it is they are trying to ease. 

	Windows also has a problem which linux at least
doesnt, which is that windows makes it much harder to become
savvy. The information is often not there, is not given. You cant
fiddle with windows, you cant poke around its insides, because the
resources that you need are often not their. For me it is this that
makes using windows highly frustrating. All the knowledge that I have
of windows internals comes pretty much from black box poking around. I
would point out that whilst I am now a computer professional I was
forced to learn this stuff when I was not, just a user and I did not
find it a pleasant experience. 

	All this however does not cover the essential point that
windows makes it very difficult to set up any idea of security. You
cant protect vital bits of your system whilst installing
things. For instance I recently managed to junk my system when I tried
to install novell, which screwed up the boot, and which I could
stop. Other examples would be applications which reset file extension
associations, and renames them to something weird so that you have to
search through all of the file types one by one to reset them (until
you discover that you can do a search using regedit...my point about
transparancy of the system once again comes forth). 

	The last time I installed a linux system (about 4 or 5 years
ago now I guess) it wasnt easy, but at least I could get the info if I
was prepared to put the time into reading the documentation. 


	Phil
	

	



