|timlee@netcom.com.DELETE-THIS.BIT (Timothy J. Lee) wrote:
|>Really, it shouldn't take much to solve the DLL mess.  
|>Just enforce
|>the idea that only service packs or patches distributed by 
|>the OS
|>vendor should be updating the system directories; 
|>applications should
|>keep out of the system directories when installing 
|>themselves.  And
|>make incompatible versions have different names.
|
|What about when the library is application code shared 
|among a number of applications? Do you put it in the system 
|directory anyway? Force all the applications into the same 
|directory? Have the applications load the library using an 
|explicit path, relative or otherwise? 

If the applications are from the same vendor, that vendor can
presumably control the installation directory structure for
that set of applications in order to allow for safe sharing
of the libraries.  For applications from different vendors,
the safe / conservative way is to not try to share the
libraries with each other -- a bit of wasted disk space is
much less of a problem than mysterious bugs caused by incompatible
shared libraries.

