>Exactly. It should not be possible to remove the library 
>whan any of the applications using it are present in the 
>system. It should be possible to overwrite it only with a 
>newer version that supported the same interfaces. It should 
>be possible for the applications using the library to 
>access it independently of its location.
>
>It certainly should not be the case that when another, 
>totally seperate application looks for a file, it 
>automatically looks in the same directory as the library 
>because the directory is on a global, shared path. The 
>library should not be stored in a system directory or 
>general purpose applications directory with hundreds of 
>other unconnected files.
>
>I have heard of various Unix approaches to this problem, 
>but I remain to be convinced that they aren't all kludges 
>and workarounds. I would expect that a workable solution to 
>the problem would apply to all libraries used by all 
>applications.

The problem transcends operating systems; simply put, how does one
guarantee that the functionality in Library Lib is available
for Program Prog?  (Or, another library)

One possible, but ugly, solution, might be the Dynamic Matching
approach. Every call is encoded into a packet, with arguments
that can be type-checked (e.g., they are derived from a common
base class and passed in as pointers or something); the callee
would be responsible for checking the arguments, matching
them against what it wants the arguments to be (supplying
defaults as required), and ultimately accepting the packet
and doing something reasonable with it, or rejecting it and throwing
an exception. As a software package evolves, the callee arguments
will change, but no callee or callee argument is ever deleted.
(It's ugly because it will yield dead code after awhile, and the
current C++ syntax can't handle defaults within the middle
of a call. Memory mangement might be a bit of a nightmare.)

Another, slightly more reasonable, approach would be the inclusion
of a version number with the library, so that the loader of said
library can have some assurance of what version of the protocol
expected of that library is available. The library, of course,
would never be deleted in this case; subsequent versions
would be differently named, or available in some fashin that
may transcend traditional 8.3 file naming methods.; in other
words, someone might ask for the COMMDLG library, version
32.8.A, and this version would be available, as well as 32.8.B,
32.9.C, and 33. Or, the program may simply ask for version
32 (which would be sloppy, because the changes between 32.1.A
and 32.9.C may involve significant, though not major, changes
in behavior).

Or one can simply load...and hope.

Ultimately, it all boils down to trust; do we have enough trust
in our libraries (and the include files shipped with them, in the
case of the developers seeking to build applications
using those libraries)?

How would we engender such trust, both at the source code and
at the binary code level? (If one, for example, includes
a new virtual declaration within a C++ class, any class derived
therefrom will have to be recumpiled *except when the new virtual
is placed at the end of the class*. As you might see, this way
lies madness.)

One nice issue about "free source code" is that one no longer has
to worry quite as much about binary code incompatibilities; one
recompiles everything and voila.

