In article <37D5895B.2C249CBA@pitt.edu>,
	Jacob Gladish <gladish+@pitt.edu> writes:
> ...
>> Stephen Tweedie is the right person to ask. On an Alpha, the issue is
>> the PCI address space being smaller than main memory. The Sparc64 port
>> has the same issue, and we need a general solution that will also
>> eventually work with the Merced, etc etc.
> 
> 
> I don't understand this. If the address space is smaller than main
> memory, then what will increasing main memory size do?

main memory isn't the problem.  the problem is that the PCI bus only
provides for 32 bit addresses, so any peripheral connected over this bus 
has problems addressing memory that is outside that range.  there was a
similar problem with older Intel systems (ISA?).  Older Linux kernels
could be configured to keep system buffers within the address range
accessible for ISA peripherals.  Maybe this type of code should be
revived id the need for more than 2GB memory becomes poressing.

