This is incorrect.  The -mieee switch is a compiler option; it changes the
code generated by gcc.

The Alpha by itself is not fully IEEE conformant.  It will generate a fp
trap on NaN, Inf, underflow, etc.  The -mieee flag causes gcc to insert
trap barriers in the code, so the kernel can recover from traps and
emulate the IEEE behavior in software.

It also incurs a performance penalty, so unless you need strict IEEE
behavior you are better off avoiding it.

Jeff

On Wed, 8 Sep 1999, Emile van bergen wrote:
> > Also, why is this flag not documented in the gcc man page?  Are there
> > any other flags that might be of relevance to me?
> 
> It's not exactly a flag. -mfoo tells the linker to link against libfoo.a
> (statically) / libfoo.so (dynamically).




