Valid complaints.  In fact, a few years ago during the demise of the Amiga I
heard similar complaints from a Commodore official, that the Amiga users
actually had a hand in shortening the life.

>In myself I've noticed a pattern: I usually find
>things to like in all the platforms I use, but am strongly tempted to
>abandon those whose advocates call me an idiot for also liking
>something else. I used the Amiga, the Mac, the Atari ST, OS/2, and
>Linux in their early days, but was compelled to drop them all as soon
>as their user bases grew too religious. After spending so many years
>working with Unix, I find nothing more annoying than having some
>ignorant loudmouth who discovered Linux last month tell me I don't
>know anything about Unix, just because I happen like Windows as well.

Things are upside down right now.  The zealotry is inevitable, but it is only
half about the attributes of the OS, be it Linux or Unix or Windows.  The other
half is about politics.  Right now the business practices of Microsoft have so
skewed the entire industry that "I happen like Windows as well" becomes a
synonym for "I support the conniving of the Microsoft leadership."  Zealots (or
to use the less pejorative "advocates") know that the strategy and obsession of
Gates and therefore his company has nothing to do with just getting along or
"happening" to prefer one technology over another.  What has been shown in
depositions, testimony, and the infamous email trail is an individual, Gates,
who hammers any perceived threat to Microsoft until it goes away, and he has the
resources to accomplish that goal.  

Were Linux (Unix, Mac, OS/2, Amiga) users to cease constant pressure, it's
probable that the Internet would become one big Microsoft LAN.  That's not such
a ridiculous dystopian view considering that each and every change in Microsoft
networking technology has been to attempt replacement of recognized, standard
protocols with others controlled by Microsoft, proprietary, difficult for others
to duplicate.  In cases where standards have prevailed, TCP/IP for instance, it
is despite the best efforts of this company.  The only call for standards has
been in areas where standards temporarily benefit Microsoft, e.g., where
penetration is small and someone else or no one controls.  

We can witness this now in the number of Internet functions available only if
one is using Internet Explorer.

Left unchecked, I and many others fear that those using other than Microsoft
products would find themselves unable to communicate, isolated, precluded from
making non-Microsoft choices.  "This site only viewable with Internet Explorer 4
or better," could very easily apply to the entire Internet.  On the basis of all
evidence I've seen throughout Microsoft's history, this is precisely the
business plan of this company, one with capitalization larger than half of
Europe.

It's a little difficult to confine ones self to the attributes of the software
in such a threatening climate.

>Never mind the fact that I was *USING* Linux years before this guy had
>ever heard of it.
>

I know.  I'm new to Linux (1 year), but I've been through the wars, believe me.




