On Thu, 09 Sep 1999 09:48:30 +0200, Alex Rhomberg <rhomberg@ife.ee.ethz.ch>
 wrote:
>AFAIK, the first one was EV57, which means that process technology
>(5=0.35u) is as usual way behind design (7 = 7th design step or so)
>The EV67 is the same processor in a better process (6=0.25u), but it
>still behind (state of the art is about 0.18u and copper). This is the
>main reason why the EV67 is faster than EV57

Come one people, let's get our facts straight ;-)
The first number of the two is the design generation, while the second is
the process generation.

I.e.: 21164 = EV5 (5th gen processor/built with 5th gen process)
      21164a= EV56
      21264 = EV6
      21264a= EV67 (6th gen processor/built with 7th gen process)
And maybe soon
      21364 = EV7 with a ~100M transistors :_)


I don't know if there was an EV57, and actually the 'design' seems to lag
the fab process (that is if you take the numbers assigned to a particular
generation of process and design to be directly comparable, which of
course isn't really the case)

Somebody else could comment on the link between the fab process number and
the feature size. My recollection is that EV5_6_ was 0.35u and EV6_7_ is
0.25u. The feature sizes of the Alpha seems to lag those from Intel and most
DRAM fabs. Samsung might change that though...

Cheers

Conrad

