sean@forget.about.it (Sen  Donnchadha) writes:

>I agree with him. DLL Hell is a popular scapegoat whenever someone is
>too lazy to put effort into diagnosing the problem. DLL conflicts do
>happen, but much less frequently than the FUDsters would have you
>believe. And neither Linux nor any other OS has a real solution.

As I don't use Windows, I have no idea whether DLL Hell is real or
not.

However, GNU/Linux actually does have a real solution to what I
understand the problem to be: library versioning and symbol
versioning.

Library versioning permits a wholesale replacement of a library
without disturbing old programs.

Symbol versioning permits you to ensure that a shared library
continues to support old interfaces even as you enhance it to define
new interfaces and to change the old ones.

Granted, you could still have problems if you have bugs in the old
library, programs come to depend upon those bugs, and you then fix the
bugs.  While either form of versioning could be used to avoid these
problems, you probably wouldn't think to do it.

These sorts of problems can in part be avoided by maintaining a clear
understanding of the difference between the specification and the
implementation.  I know that in the old days DOS programmers had a
hard time maintaining that distinction, in part because the
specification was inadequate.  I don't know whether Windows carries on
that tradition.
