In article <37d93940.34897269@news.shore.net>,
	sean@forget.about.it (Se�n � Donnchadha) writes:
> I agree with him. DLL Hell is a popular scapegoat whenever someone is
> too lazy to put effort into diagnosing the problem. DLL conflicts do
> happen, but much less frequently than the FUDsters would have you
> believe. And neither Linux nor any other OS has a real solution.

Allowing 3rd parties to modify shared OS functionality IS a basic design
flaw.  It's almost trivial, even with a MS-type OS, to design a scheme such
that 3rd party apps could override or enhance basic DLL functions but not
effect any other app or OS facility.

The fact that this was not done is curious.

BTW, Unix and other OSs _do_ address the problem in various ways - none of
which cause version problems or side effects.


