Steve Sheldon wrote:
> 
> "Nik Simpson" <nik@hiwaay.net> writes:
> 
> >Steve Sheldon wrote in message ...
> >> The definition of 64-bit clean means more than just that the OS will run
> >>atop a 64-bit processor.  Windows NT 4.0 also runs atop a 64-bit Alpha, but
> >>that doesn't make it 64-bit clean.
> 
> >For example, a true 64bit OS has the capability to address very large (>4GB)
> >of physical memory, it's APIs will use 64 bit integers instead of 32bit etc.
> 
>  To be fair it does appear from the Alpha Linux FAQ that it does indeed use
> 64-bit integers.
> 
>  However, it is limited to 2 gigs of addressable memory, which is a 32-bit
> limitation.

Steve,

maybe I can clear this up a bit (I am working with two GNU/Linux Alpha
Systems)

Linux Alpha is limited to 2 GB *physical* memory. It can have more
*virtual* or as you put it addressable memory.

The 2GB *physical* limit is because 32-Bit PCI cards can only address 32
bit of memory. It doesn't matter if the PCI bus itself supports 64 Bit,
Any 32 bit PCI card that wants to do DMA as bus master can only write to
32 Bit addressable memory.

TRU64 servers can have more RAM because it's possible to distinguish
between PCI-addressable mem and other. 

Linux-Alpha is 64 bit clean in that *pointers* and *size-critical
numbers* are 64 bit. There is no need that all ints be 64 bit. The only
critical number that comes to mind is in timeval (the number of seconds
since 1970, 32 bit gives y2038 problem)

To summarize:
Linux-Alpha is 64 bit, as pointers are 64 bit memory and addressable
(virtual) mem is > 4GB. The 2GB limit for *physical* RAM is due to the
limitations of PCI busmaster cards.

- Alex



