Hi folks,
since I didn't have the c't-issue with me yesterday and I read the article 2
weeks ago (without my glasses ;-)) I looked it up again and have to make a
correction regarding the FP-benchmarks. Unfortunately the article isn't
online and since it is in german You wouldn't understand it anyway. ;) The
Alpha CPU was in a Compaq DS20 with 2GB-RAM. They used egcs-1.1.2 and did
the SPECint95 and SPECfp95 and here are the results:
SPECint95 (egcs-1.1.2)
Intel PIII/600    18,9
Athlon 600        20,7
Alpha 500        18,1
SPECfp95 (egcs-1.1.2)
Intel PIII/600    12,4
Athlon 600        17,5
Alpha 500        22,8
When using Digital Visual Fortran V6.0a it looked like this
SPECfp95
Intel PIII/600    14,7
Athlon 600        21,0
Alpha 500        39,6 (wow, very impressive)
The other SPEC benches were done with Microsoft MSC 6.0
SPECint95
Intel PIII/600    20,0
Athlon 600        21,6
Alpha 500        21,2
or Intel Fortran and C/C++ Compilers respectively, so there was no Alpha in
these but the Athlon easily bet the Intel CPUs. :)
So when it comes to FP performance the 21264 is definetly the ticket,
especially when using DEC Fortran. We are in a Linux newsgroup here, so the
egcs results are probably even more interesting and then the Athlon looks
quite good, doesn't he? Sometimes it is hard to reproduce the results
anyway, since you never know all the compiler options used for the
benchmarks. When you take the price-tag into account the Athlon is a good
choice right now, but hopefully there will be positive effect on the Alpha
prices because of the shared slot design. Maybe we will see some priceworthy
Alpha boards within the next months. Compared to SUN, SGI or some other
(mainframe)machines the Alpha workstations or servers are really 'cheap',
which you already mentioned. By the way, at the University we get about 50 %
rebate on these machines, so they get affordable. ;)
Dirk


