Same here, except that I'm on the development side of things. I worked
in the Unix world for most of a decade, then switched in late '95 to
Windows programming.

In my experience, when you look at operating systems and the
applications available for them as a single whole, Unix systems are on
average far worse than Windows systems in nearly every way -
price/performance, price/capability, user interface, and even
reliability.

Yes, I said "reliability"; Unix kernels *SOMETIMES* have the edge over
Windows, but Unix apps are on the whole of much lower quality. And the
kernel advantage comes straight from the Unix vendor's control over
the hardware configuration. The latter hypothesis is supported by the
fact that commercial Unixes for standard x86 boxes have always been
relatively unreliable despite being limited to small subsets of PC
hardware.

So the excellent reliability of Linux and other free Unixes is the
exception here, not the rule. But mark my words: Linux reliability
will drop like a rock the minute hardware vendors start writing their
own drivers for it.

>
>Man at every turn the Linux zealots just keep spewing the hatred and
>turning up to be wrong.
>

I'm convinced that zealots have contributed greatly to the failure of
many platforms. In myself I've noticed a pattern: I usually find
things to like in all the platforms I use, but am strongly tempted to
abandon those whose advocates call me an idiot for also liking
something else. I used the Amiga, the Mac, the Atari ST, OS/2, and
Linux in their early days, but was compelled to drop them all as soon
as their user bases grew too religious. After spending so many years
working with Unix, I find nothing more annoying than having some
ignorant loudmouth who discovered Linux last month tell me I don't
know anything about Unix, just because I happen like Windows as well.
Never mind the fact that I was *USING* Linux years before this guy had
ever heard of it.




