On Wed, 08 Sep 1999 13:31:10 GMT, sylvest <ssylvest@home.com> wrote:

>What does a solenoid do in valve actuation?

Typically it moves the valve handle. As in on/off.
All a solenoid is, is a coil through which you run
a current, and a core which is attached to the device
that you want to move. Run the current through the
coil in one direction and a magnetic field is
generated which pushes on the core, which pushes the
handle. Switch the wires around and run the current
through the coil the other way and the field, and
action is reversed, ie. it pulls on the handle.




