nick@faust.sec.baileynm.com (Nick Manka) writes:

>In article <jau2p97x8e.fsf@gatsby.u-net.com>,
>	David Wragg <dpw@doc.ic.ac.uk> writes:

>>> Mind you, Tru64 UNIX is a damn solid BSD. I like it a lot, it's
>>> just a tad expensive.
>> 
>> Tru64 is not a BSD. Ultrix, DEC's original Unix, was a BSD. Tru64,
>> AKA Digital Unix, AKA OSF/1, is a SVR4 derivative, IIRC.

>The Digital UNIX nee Tru64 Unix machine that I'm logged into right
>now is most definitely a derivative of 4.3BSD on Mach.  It has some
>SYSV compatibility libraries, but that does not make it SVR4.
>Whether that remains true past V5.0 is up in the air.

 OSF/1 was indeed a cross between BSD and SVR4.  The supposed idea at the
time was to create a Unix from the ground up that contained the best of both
worlds.  By doing this, all the various vendors would then ship machines
which ran OSF/1 and there would no longer be Unix fragmentation.

 Unfortunately DEC was the only vendor which was part of OSF who followed
through on the idea.  They eventually gave up on the notion of OSF and
renamed it to "Digital Unix", and it's now called "Tru64" as a marketing
gimmick.

 Even though as far as I recall the first version of OSF/1 1.3 I worked on
on an Alpha was 64-bit.  Perhaps not all of it, can't remember.  I just
remember having to fix some of my Ultrix code to get it to work.
