On Tue, 7 Sep 1999, Steve Sheldon wrote:
> >Also, don't forget that Alpha-Linux is 64bit clean, and NT4 is _not_ 
> 
>  Linux is not 64-bit.  It's still heavily dependent on the Intel
> architecture.  There are some small 64-bitisms for support of SPARC and
> Alpha, but it's not by any definition 64-bit clean.

You're talking about Linux 1.x, right?  Yeah, it was Intel-centric.  There
were ports to other architectures but the patches required were ugly.

2.0 was a complete rewrite, making Linux architecture-neutral.  There is
architecture-specific code for SPARC, Alpha, x86 etc. but the wide
majority of Linux kernel code now compiles/runs perfectly on both 32-bit
and 64-bit targets.

> >	Linux, because life's too short for a buggy OS.
> 
>  Odd that you use Linux then.

Linux has bugs, like any OS... however Linux kernel bugs are
detected/patched/fixed at a rate faster than I've ever seen for any OS
development.  The end result is high-quality, stable code releases (though
some releases are admittedly better than others).

Jeff





