> driver's information through /dev/sndstat, the kernel modules don't,
> as expected. From that point on, the behaviour is identical: If I try
> to play a midi file with 'mplay <midi file>', it doesn't do anything,
> but doesn't return to command line until I press Ctrl-C. When I want
> to play .wav's or .au's, the machine is struck dead. It totally
> crashes: not just a kernel panic or similar, it freezes and doesn't
> react on mouse, keyboard, external pings. Sometimes the speakers
> generate a continous harsh humming then.

The Ensoniq 1370 and 1371 chipsets are not PCI compliant. I have tried
to use both in my 164UX machine, and they simply do not work (they
have the symptoms you described in your post). The deal with that
seems to be that these chipsets cannot handle living in a machine with
more than the small number of interrupts provided by the Intel
architecture. Don't you just love hardware designed to use the bugs,
er... I mean _features_ of the x86?

I suggest the SoundBlaster 16. It works flawlessly on my Alpha and has
pretty good sound quality.


Regards,
Constantine Vetoshev



