> Is that some sort of standard laptop thingy?

Well, 'standard' and laptop in the same sentence...? Ho humm... But yes,
the drive is used in some laptops. It is (still?) manufactured by
Citizen, model W1D. You can get one for $35 at e.g. http://www.halted.com/

> where would I get a cable of that sort?
> Does anyone know of any documents that give pinouts for this thing and
> the 4 pin header (which i assume from docs is power for 3.5 inch SCSI
> drive) on the SCSI riser card?

The pinouts for the floppy drive are as follows (26-pin ribbon cable, as
opposed to the normal 34-pin one). FWIW, IMHO it _should_ be possible to
connect a regular drive to than one, provided you manufacture a suitable
cable and manage to get the additional +12v from somewhere else.

1 VDD 		14 /STEP
2 /INDEX 	15 GND
3 VDD 		16 /WRDATA
4 DR0 		17 GND
5 VDD 		18 /WGATE
6 DISKCH 	19 GND
7 N/C 		20 /TRK0
8 N/C 		21 GND
9 N/C 		22 /WP
10 MTR0 	23 GND
11 N/C 		24 /RDDATA
12 DIR 		25 GND
13 N/C 		26 /HDSEL


You can get the pinouts (for a regular floppy drive too) at

ftp://n293.ede.telekabel.euronet.nl/pub/multia/citizen-floppy.gif

and

ftp://n293.ede.telekabel.euronet.nl/pub/multia/std-floppy.gif

I don't happen to have the pinouts of the 4-pin header you describe,
although some probing with a multimeter could possibly reveal what
you're looking for... ;-)


Hope this helps.

Best regards,


Emile van Bergen (emile@n293.ede.telekabel.euronet.nl).




