In comp.os.linux.advocacy Steve Sheldon <sheldon@visi.com> wrote:
:  People don't buy SCO because they're all dang fired up about running Unix. 
: They buy it because some application that they need runs only on SCO.
:
:  Get those application vendors on the Linux bandwagon, and yes you'll kill
: SCO.


Without wishing any harm to SCO, I think they are at a crossroads of
sorts, for the following reasons.

First, much SCO software *does* run on Linux, via SCO<->Linux
compatibility libraries; I don't know much about them firsthand, but
have heard a number of success stories and very few complaints.

Second, if I produced commerical server products, I would want to
support *all* common Unix variants, including Linux, preferably out of
a common code base.  (And, yes, I'd support NT too, but it would not
be my recommended platform for any server product.)  I see no reason
to prefer one Unix variant over others when they are all pretty
largely compatible.

Third, SCO has seen the writing on the wall.  It certainly appears to
understand the symbiotic interaction that exists between free and
proprietary Unices (www.sco.com/linux/).  I actually think that *most*
x86 Unices will go open-source or disappear, as commercial vendors
concede the low end of the market to Linux and FreeBSD, and
concentrate on the higher-end and semi-proprietary hardware.  This
puts SCO between a rock and a hard place, since they are the Unix
vendor with the most at stake on the x86 platform.

Finally, SCO has at present no assured business model.  Unlike Sun, HP
or IBM, it doesn't sell hardware.  Software is pretty much its only
business.

If I were them I'd start thinking about some serious refocusing right
about now.  That might include any or all of the following, to the
extent they're not already doing them:

  *  supporting additional CPU architectures

  *  offering value-added services (support, testing, etc.) 
     built on top of Linux *and* UnixWare

  *  maximizing the Linux compatibility of all of its offerings

  *  positioning itself as a value-added "upgrade" to entry-level
     Linux or FreeBSD systems

  *  contributing to the infrastructure (Wine, KDE, Gnome, XFree,
     Java, etc.) needed to gain parity with NT in two of NT's
     strongest areas: (a) application support, and (b) ease of
     developing "one-off" data-centric, in-house applications

  *  if all else fails, consider developing its own branded 
     Linux distribution.


Joe



