In comp.os.linux.advocacy Chad Mulligan <cmulligan@hipcrime.vocab.org> wrote:

:>Not if they want to use MS software, which is often deliberately
:>designed not to work with older versions of other MS software, in
:>order to force people to upgrade.
:>

: Prove this assertion.

Oh, come on.  You know better.  It is common knowledge among software
developers for the Windows platform, especially those who freelance
and/or work in companies where multiple versions of any given product
are supported.

Examples: many post-1994 releases of Visual Basic and Visual FoxPro
broke programs written using older versions of these same products. 
Furthermore, all were vulnerable to DLL changes caused by IE and
Office.  This was a HUGE problem given that at the time this problem
started, many consultants (myself included) would sell custom apps
built using these tools, which would run just fine for one, two or
three years, and then gratuitously die.  Invariably, I'd find out that
the event which triggered the "failure" of my programs was the
installation of IE, Office, or another custom app built with a
different version of the development tool in question.  Reinstalling
my app would often fix it, but break IE, Office, and/or the other
third-party apps.

I spent two years fixing, rebuilding and testing stuff and reassuring
my clients that someday Microsoft, the largest software company in
the world, would "get it right." 

It never did.  W2K will mark its first serious attempt to chase down
the DLL Hell problem.  I wish MS well on this attempt, if only for the
sake of its beleaguered customers, but it remains to be seen whether
it will succeed.  I'm not betting my business on it.

I still do VB development, but only in-house, and only for clients who
have standardized on VB, a tool I no longer recommend for any but the
most trivial of applications.

See the deja.com archives for the newsgroups comp.databases.xbase.fox
and comp.languages.basic.visual right around the time of each new
release for more "war stories" than you'll ever want to see.  Also,
some of the incompatibilities (particularly between VB4/32 and VB5,
and between FPW2.6 and VFP) are, or at least were, documented on the
Microsoft Knowledge Base. 

Linux is still new and unfamiliar to many of my clients, but slowly
that's changing, and as it does, I expect to a great deal more
development for this platform.  I hope to develop a business model
based on customizing free software to the specific (and often highly
proprietary) needs of my clients.  I have no idea of how this will work. 

I have to admit that Microsoft development tools, for all their other
faults, were very good for my pocketbook.  But they were not good for
my clients.  Free software will probably meet their needs much better
- not because of the price, but because of freedom.  They will remain
in complete control of their software and their data; anything I do
can be understood and if necessary modified by someone else, or vice
versa.  But it will be somewhat of a challenge to make money as a
freelance consultant in this field until all of the implications of
this newfound freedom work themselves out.  I do believe there is
money to be made, but I have no guaranteed formula to find it.


Joe



