There is a phrase, "no-one ever got fired for buying Microsoft".
(Originally this phrase was about IBM, but times have changed).

Is this still true? (Was it ever?)

Consider the position of an executive who has just bought a
large Alpha system (and some Alpha boxes cost $100,000), running 
Windows NT. Microsoft have just decided not to support NT on Alpha 
any more, so the executive has just bought some rather expensive 
paperweights. Does anyone know of anyone in this position, whose
job might be now a little, how shall we say, precarious?

All is not lost, however. Linux runs on Alpha, and will always be
supported -- because of the power of Open Source, it matters a lot
less if the vendor walks away from their OS. Linux is a better OS
anyway, so it's a blessing in disguise.


